// EXPECT: FAIL
// ERROR_CONTAINS: ANUBIS_INVALID_ARGUMENT
// RUNTIME: exercise via `anubis run` (not `anubis check` — this is a runtime panic;
// `anubis check` on this file passes clean today, verified 2026-07-26).
// PENDING (fails today): repeat("x", -1) silently clamps a negative count to 0 and
// returns "" (compiler/src/backends/run.rs anubis_repeat, ~line 2082,
// `n.as_i64().max(0)`). A negative repeat count is a caller bug (e.g. a miscomputed
// length), not a meaningful request for "zero repetitions" — the language's fail-closed
// posture says surface it rather than silently produce an empty (but well-typed and
// therefore unremarkable-looking) result.
// See scratchpad/fleet_20260726/sonnet5c_stdlib_failclosed.md for the full audit
// (this entry also records the counter-argument: some languages treat negative
// repeat/multiply counts as an unsurprising "zero" convention).
fn main() {
    print(repeat("x", -1));
}
